  <content>
    <section>
      <title> Different Exponential Representations</title>
      <para>A table of values is shown below for a function \(g(x)=(\frac{1}{2})^x\), which represents exponential decay.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
        <tgroup cols="8"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>\(x\)</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>–3</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>–2</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>–1</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>0</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>1</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>2</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <emphasis>
                <para>3</para>
              </emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <para>\(g(x) = (\frac{1}{2})^x\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>8</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>4</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>2</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>1</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(\frac12\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(\frac14\)</para>
            </entry>
            <entry>
              <para>\(\frac18\)</para>
            </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
      <para>When these points are graphed, they show the decay because the function graphed decreases from left to right:</para>
      <para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_10_05_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
     </para>
      <title> Try It: Different Exponential Representations</title>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Make a table for the function \(f(x)=4^x\). Then graph the function.</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Here is how to make a table and then graph the exponential function:</para>
            <para>First, substitute values in for \(x\) to find the values of \(f(x)\).</para>
            <table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
              <tgroup cols="6"><tbody><row>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                      <para>\(x\)</para>
                    </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                      <para>0</para>
                    </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                      <para>1</para>
                    </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                      <para>2</para>
                    </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                      <para>3</para>
                    </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <emphasis>
                      <para>4</para>
                    </emphasis>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              <row>
                  <entry>
                    <para> \(f(x)=4^x\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(4^0=1\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(4^1=4\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(4^2=16\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(4^3=64\)</para>
                  </entry>
                  <entry>
                    <para>\(4^4=256\)</para>
                  </entry>
                </row>
              </tbody></tgroup></table>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_10_05_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
           </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>